39,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1564},{"week":15,"opponent":"BUF","home":true,"pf":10.0,"pa":17.0,"off_snaps":56,"epa_per_play":-0.401,"pass_rate":0.75,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6905,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1539},{"week":16,"opponent":"NYJ","home":false,"pf":10.0,"pa":16.0,"off_snaps":66,"epa_per_play":-0.1762,"pass_rate":0.6212,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4857,"def_epa_per_play":-0.206},{"week":17,"opponent":"BAL","home":false,"pf":10.0,"pa":28.0,"off_snaps":49,"epa_per_play":-0.4222,"pass_rate":0.551,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5556,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1083}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2021,"team":"WAS","distance":0.3877,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"TB","distance":0.454,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"},{"season":2020,"team":"LAC","distance":0.5068,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Shane Steichen"},{"season":2021,"team":"JAX","distance":0.5189,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"CAR","distance":0.5397,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"}],"defense":[{"season":2021,"team":"GB","distance":0.7517,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"LA","distance":0.7838,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Raheem Morris"},{"season":2022,"team":"LAC","distance":0.7883,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2021,"team":"LAC","distance":0.81,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2018,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.886,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Steelers went 8-8 in 2019 and were outscored 303 to 289. The defense did most of the work, finishing 3rd in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 32nd in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits were the 3rd lowest 13 personnel usage and the fewest defenders in the box in the league.","offense":"The Steelers offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 3rd lowest 13 personnel usage in the league. Mason Rudolph ran the passing game, with Diontae Johnson leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Steelers had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 5th or better in all five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was sack rate, where it led the league, and it had no real weak spot. T.J. Watt was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Mark Barron, who had 3 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they had the fewest defenders in the box in the league.","special":"The Steelers were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 94% of field goals, which was one of the best in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the quarterback, about 17% of the money on the books. Their secondary was among the youngest in the league.","players":"The quarterback job was split, with Mason Rudolph and Devlin Hodges both getting meaningful snaps. Targets were spread around, with Diontae Johnson and James Washington leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by James Conner and Benny Snell. On defense, T.J. Watt led the team with 14.5 sacks and Joe Haden picked off 5 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2021 Commanders and the 2023 Buccaneers under Dave Canales. Their defense looked most like the 2021 Packers and the 2023 Rams under Raheem Morris.","trends":"The offense went from 5th in EPA per play in 2018 to 32nd in 2019, a big step back. The defense moved from 18th in EPA allowed per play in 2018 to 3rd in 2019, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was Cover 6 rate, which went from 2% to 10%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2019. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}